# Before Installation
Many beginners think they are installing one software called MySQL

Actually, we're installing two important components ikkada::
𝟭. 𝗠𝘆𝗦𝗤𝗟 𝗦𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗲𝗿 - 𝗧𝗵𝗶𝘀 𝗶𝘀 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗮𝗰𝘁𝘂𝗮𝗹 𝗱𝗮𝘁𝗮𝗯𝗮𝘀𝗲 -> 𝗜𝘁 𝘀𝘁𝗼𝗿𝗲𝘀 𝘆𝗼𝘂𝗿 𝗱𝗮𝘁𝗮
Without MySQL Server, nothing works -> The Brain of the Database

𝟮. 𝗠𝘆𝗦𝗤𝗟 𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗸𝗯𝗲𝗻𝗰𝗵 -> 𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗸𝗯𝗲𝗻𝗰𝗵 𝗶𝘀 𝗷𝘂𝘀𝘁 𝗮 𝗴𝗿𝗮𝗽𝗵𝗶𝗰𝗮𝗹 𝗶𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗳𝗮𝗰𝗲
It helps us
- Write SQL
- Create tables
- View data
- Execute queries -> The Remote Control for MySQL Server

╰┈➤Step 1: Check Your System
Before installing, verify:
-> Windows 10 or Windows 11
-> At least 4 GB RAM (8 GB recommended)
-> Around 2 GB free disk space
-> Administrator access

╰┈➤Step 2: Download MySQL Installer
Open your browser -> Go to the official MySQL website: https://dev.mysql.com/downloads/installer/
You'll see two installer options
Option 1: mysql-installer-web-community - Small download (~2 MB)
Downloads components during installation -> Requires Internet

Option 2 (Recommended) - mysql-installer-community
Large download (~400 MB+) -> Contains everything -> Works even without Internet after downloading -> Take this

╰┈➤Step 3: Start Installation
Double-click: mysql-installer-community.exe
Allow Windows permission if prompted-> Click Yes

╰┈➤Step 4: Choose Setup Type
You'll see several options -> Developer Default
Includes
MySQL Server
MySQL Workbench
Shell
Connectors
Documentation

Choose: Developer Default -> Click Next

╰┈➤Step 5: Install Required Software
Sometimes MySQL asks to install:
Microsoft Visual C++ Redistributable
.NET Framework -> Click Execute

Wait until everything installs
Click -> Next

╰┈➤Step 6: Install Products
You'll see something like
MySQL Server 9.x
MySQL Workbench
MySQL Shell
Connectors -> Click Execute -> Wait-> This may take several minutes
When everything shows -> Complete -> Click Next

╰┈➤Step 7: Configure MySQL Server
Now configuration begins(This is important)
Config Type -> Choose Development Computer
Why?: Because you're installing MySQL for learning on your own laptop. ->This setting uses fewer system resources -> Click Next
Step 8: Connectivity

You'll see
TCP/IP
Port : 3306 -> Don't change it (Port 3306 is MySQL's default communication port) -> Think of it as MySQL's phone number
Applications use this port to communicate with the server -> Click Next

╰┈➤Step 9: Authentication Method
You'll see two options-> Use Strong Password Encryption -> This is the recommended and more secure option -> Click Next
Step ╰┈➤10: Create Root Password
Now you'll create the administrator password
Example
Username
root Password: Algonex@DOB (Choose a password you can remember) -> Avoid weak passwords like 123456 or password -> Click Next
╰┈➤Step 11: Windows Service -> Keep the default settings
Service Name
MySQL90 (or a similar version-based name)
This allows the server to start automatically when Windows starts -> Click Next
Step 12: Apply Configuration -> You'll now see several tasks

Writing Configuration, Starting Server, Applying Security, Finishing Configuration -> Click Execute -> Wait until every step shows a green check mark -> Click Finish
╰┈➤Step 13: Finish Installation -> Click Next -> Finish -> Congratulations! MySQL is now installed.

╰┈➤Step 14: Open MySQL Workbench
Search Windows for MySQL Workbench -> Open it
You'll see: Local Instance MySQL -> Double-click it

╰┈➤Step 15: Login
You'll be prompted for the password
Enter the root password you created during installation -> Click OK
If everything is correct, you'll see the MySQL Workbench SQL Editor -> Congratulations  -> You are now connected to your first MySQL Server -> SELECT VERSION();

╰┈➤You installed:
MySQL Server -> Stores Databases
and
MySQL Workbench -> Lets You Control the Server

